 \paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
 
