   400	\section{The first test (October 2026)}\label{sec:eval1}
   401	\paragraph{Claims.} The original claim was locked and timestamped on 17 October 2026, before the rule was implemented. At $\beta = \evone{0.05}$, the median position, and on 40 fresh seeds, its \emph{rule clause} required, for each confirmatory family, a one-sided 95\% lower bound of R3's gross efficiency of at least \evone{0.90}, a one-sided 95\% upper bound of its gain of at most \evone{0.30}, and audit cost within budget on every seed. Its \emph{baseline clause} required, for each of B2 and B3, a confirmatory family in which either the paired inflation-caused efficiency-loss difference exceeded \evone{0.05} or the baseline's gain exceeded \evone{1.0}, at the 98.75\% level. Amendment 1, registered after the baselines' development results showed that the baseline clause was unlikely to pass, added an amended claim on the public identity-replacement family D2+H3: the same absolute part as the rule clause (gross-efficiency lower bound at least \evone{0.90}, gain upper bound at most \evone{0.30}, budget compliance) and paired gain reductions of at least \evone{0.10} against B2 and B3 at the 98.75\% level, plus the H2 rule clause. After a development test killed the first rule, Amendment 3 registered one successor, R3, before its implementation.
